  "structural_leakage_pairs_first_candle_after_t": 0,
  "structural_leakage_removals_dated_after_t": 0,
  "leakage_tolerated": 0,
  "venue_disagreement_panel_undercounts_tightest": 2,
  "venue_disagreement_panel_overcounts_tightest": 7,
  "method": "two tests. The structural one is exact and decisive: no counted pair may have a first candle after t and no removal dated after t may enter a trailing feature - the only two routes by which future information could reach a feature. The venue comparison then asks whether the route count matches what Binance serves with endTime = t, so the venue itself cannot return a post-t candle. Disagreements are classified by direction rather than called leakage: a pair that exists but has not traded for weeks makes the panel look high without any future information being used, and a pair whose window starts later in our table than at the venue makes it look low, which is conservative."
 },
